    "question_hi": "Why is Node.js called single-threaded? Explain Node.js Architecture & Event Loop.",
    "answer_hi": "Node.js is a runtime environment that allows us to run JavaScript outside the browser. It uses the V8 engine to execute JavaScript code. JavaScript normally runs on one main thread, which means it handles one piece of JavaScript code at a time. When Node.js starts an operation that takes time, like reading a file, it can continue doing other work instead of waiting. The event loop helps manage when callbacks for completed operations can run. Node.js also uses libuv to help with the event loop and certain background operations. The call stack keeps track of the JavaScript functions currently running. Blocking code makes the main thread wait, while non-blocking code allows it to continue working. This is why Node.js is useful for applications that handle many requests involving databases, files, or networks.\n\nSimple example: If one user is waiting for a database response, Node.js can continue handling other requests instead of waiting for that response.",
