    "answer_hi": "Bro, these three are different ways to handle tasks that take time, like reading files or getting data from an API. A callback is a function passed to another function to run later. But when callbacks are nested too much, the code becomes difficult to read. A Promise represents a result that may be available now or later. It can be pending, fulfilled, or rejected. We can handle its result using .then() and errors using .catch(). Async/await makes Promise-based code easier to read. We use async before a function and await before a Promise-based operation. We can use try...catch to handle errors.\n\n```javascript\nasync function getData() {\n  try {\n    const response = await fetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users');\n    const data = await response.json();\n    console.log(data);\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.log(error.message);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHere, await waits for each result without blocking the main JavaScript thread.",
