    "question_hi": "What are JavaScript modules (CommonJS vs ES Modules)?",
    "answer_hi": "Modules allow us to divide our code into separate files and reuse it wherever needed. For example, we can keep user-related functions in one file and import them into another file. This makes a project easier to organize, understand, and maintain. Node.js supports CommonJS modules using require() and module.exports. It also supports ES modules using import and export. CommonJS is common in existing Node.js projects, while ES modules are widely used in modern JavaScript projects. We should follow the module system configured for our project rather than mixing both randomly.\n\nExample using CommonJS:\n\n```javascript\n// math.js\nfunction add(a, b) {\n  return a + b;\n}\nmodule.exports = { add };\n\n// app.js\nconst { add } = require('./math');\nconsole.log(add(2, 3)); // Output: 5\n```\n\nRemember: module.exports shares code; require() imports it in CommonJS.",
    "question_en": "What are JavaScript modules (CommonJS vs ES Modules)?",
    "answer_en": "Modules allow us to divide our code into separate files and reuse it wherever needed. For example, we can keep user-related functions in one file and import them into another file. This makes a project easier to organize, understand, and maintain. Node.js supports CommonJS modules using require() and module.exports. It also supports ES modules using import and export. CommonJS is common in existing Node.js projects, while ES modules are widely used in modern JavaScript projects. We should follow the module system configured for our project rather than mixing both randomly.\n\nExample using CommonJS:\n\n```javascript\n// math.js\nfunction add(a, b) {\n  return a + b;\n}\nmodule.exports = { add };\n\n// app.js\nconst { add } = require('./math');\nconsole.log(add(2, 3)); // Output: 5\n```\n\nRemember: module.exports shares code; require() imports it in CommonJS.",
