    "question_hi": "What are environment variables and .env?",
    "answer_hi": "Environment variables store configuration values outside our source code. For example, a Node.js application may need a database password, database host, or JWT secret. Instead of writing these values directly in the code, we can store them in environment variables. This makes the application easier to configure across development and production environments. In local development, we commonly use a .env file with the dotenv package. We access these values using process.env. The .env file should not be committed to Git if it contains secrets. We can add it to .gitignore and provide a separate .env.example file containing placeholder values.\n\nExample .env:\n```ini\nPORT=5000\nDB_HOST=localhost\nJWT_SECRET=your_secret_value\n```\n\nIn Node.js:\n```javascript\nrequire('dotenv').config();\nconst port = process.env.PORT || 3000;\n```\n\nRemember: Keep configuration and secrets outside your source code. Never expose real secrets in a public repository.",
