    "category": "A. Node.js Fundamentals (1–30)",
    "question_hi": "What are streams in Node.js?",
    "answer_hi": "Streams are instances of EventEmitter that allow reading from or writing data chunk-by-chunk continuously instead of loading the entire dataset into memory at once.\n\nThere are 4 main types:\n1. **Readable:** e.g., `fs.createReadStream()`, `req`\n2. **Writable:** e.g., `fs.createWriteStream()`, `res`\n3. **Duplex:** both readable and writable, e.g., net socket\n4. **Transform:** modifies data as it reads/writes, e.g., `zlib.createGzip()`\n\nStreams prevent memory overflows when transferring massive files.",
    "question_en": "What are streams in Node.js?",
    "answer_en": "Streams are instances of EventEmitter that allow reading from or writing data chunk-by-chunk continuously instead of loading the entire dataset into memory at once.\n\nThere are 4 main types:\n1. **Readable:** e.g., `fs.createReadStream()`, `req`\n2. **Writable:** e.g., `fs.createWriteStream()`, `res`\n3. **Duplex:** both readable and writable, e.g., net socket\n4. **Transform:** modifies data as it reads/writes, e.g., `zlib.createGzip()`\n\nStreams prevent memory overflows when transferring massive files.",
