    "num": "40",
    "category": "B. Express.js Core (31–45)",
    "question_hi": "What are HTTP status codes and how are they used?",
    "answer_hi": "HTTP status codes tell us what happened when the server received a request. They help the frontend understand whether the request succeeded or failed. For example, 200 means the request was successful, and 201 means a new resource was created successfully. 400 means the request contains invalid data. 401 means valid authentication is missing or has failed, while 403 means the user is not allowed to access that resource. 404 means the requested resource or route was not found. 500 means an unexpected problem occurred on the server. We should choose the correct status code based on the result of the request. This makes APIs easier to understand and debug. In Express, we can set a status code using res.status() and send a response using res.json().\n\n```javascript\nres.status(200).json({ message: 'Success' });\nres.status(201).json({ message: 'Created' });\nres.status(404).json({ message: 'Not found' });\n```\n\nRemember: 2xx = success, 4xx = request or access problem, 5xx = server problem.",
    "question_en": "What are HTTP status codes and how are they used?",
    "answer_en": "HTTP status codes tell us what happened when the server received a request. They help the frontend understand whether the request succeeded or failed. For example, 200 means the request was successful, and 201 means a new resource was created successfully. 400 means the request contains invalid data. 401 means valid authentication is missing or has failed, while 403 means the user is not allowed to access that resource. 404 means the requested resource or route was not found. 500 means an unexpected problem occurred on the server. We should choose the correct status code based on the result of the request. This makes APIs easier to understand and debug. In Express, we can set a status code using res.status() and send a response using res.json().\n\n```javascript\nres.status(200).json({ message: 'Success' });\nres.status(201).json({ message: 'Created' });\nres.status(404).json({ message: 'Not found' });\n```\n\nRemember: 2xx = success, 4xx = request or access problem, 5xx = server problem.",
