    "num": "46",
    "category": "C. Database & SQL (46–60)",
    "question_hi": "How to connect MySQL with Node.js?",
    "answer_hi": "MySQL is a database used to store data in tables, such as users, products, and orders. To connect Node.js with MySQL, we can use the mysql2 package. First, we install the package and configure the database connection using the required credentials. Then, our Express routes or service functions can execute SQL queries to read or change data. For example, when the frontend requests a list of users, the backend runs a SELECT query and sends the results as JSON. We should use asynchronous database operations so the main JavaScript thread does not have to wait for the database operation to finish. In a real project, we should also handle database errors and keep credentials in environment variables instead of writing them directly in the source code.\n\nExample:\n```javascript\nconst mysql = require('mysql2/promise');\n\nconst db = mysql.createPool({\n  host: process.env.DB_HOST,\n  user: process.env.DB_USER,\n  password: process.env.DB_PASSWORD,\n  database: process.env.DB_NAME\n});\n```\n\nHere, createPool() creates a pool of reusable database connections. We can use db.execute() to run parameterized SQL queries.",
    "question_en": "How to connect MySQL with Node.js?",
    "answer_en": "MySQL is a database used to store data in tables, such as users, products, and orders. To connect Node.js with MySQL, we can use the mysql2 package. First, we install the package and configure the database connection using the required credentials. Then, our Express routes or service functions can execute SQL queries to read or change data. For example, when the frontend requests a list of users, the backend runs a SELECT query and sends the results as JSON. We should use asynchronous database operations so the main JavaScript thread does not have to wait for the database operation to finish. In a real project, we should also handle database errors and keep credentials in environment variables instead of writing them directly in the source code.\n\nExample:\n```javascript\nconst mysql = require('mysql2/promise');\n\nconst db = mysql.createPool({\n  host: process.env.DB_HOST,\n  user: process.env.DB_USER,\n  password: process.env.DB_PASSWORD,\n  database: process.env.DB_NAME\n});\n```\n\nHere, createPool() creates a pool of reusable database connections. We can use db.execute() to run parameterized SQL queries.",
