    "num": "48",
    "category": "C. Database & SQL (46–60)",
    "question_hi": "What is database schema and relationships?",
    "answer_hi": "A database schema is the design of a database. It defines what tables we need, what columns each table has, and how tables are connected. For example, an online shopping application may have users and orders tables. The users table can have an id as its primary key, while the orders table can have a user_id as a foreign key. The primary key uniquely identifies each user, and the foreign key connects an order to its user. We can use a JOIN to get related information from both tables. Good database design helps us avoid unnecessary repeated data and keeps relationships clear. Before building a project, we should decide what data we need and how it will be stored.\n\nExample:\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE users (\n  id INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  name VARCHAR(100)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE orders (\n  id INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  user_id INT,\n  FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id)\n);\n```\n\nRemember: Primary key identifies a row; foreign key connects related tables.",
    "question_en": "What is database schema and relationships?",
    "answer_en": "A database schema is the design of a database. It defines what tables we need, what columns each table has, and how tables are connected. For example, an online shopping application may have users and orders tables. The users table can have an id as its primary key, while the orders table can have a user_id as a foreign key. The primary key uniquely identifies each user, and the foreign key connects an order to its user. We can use a JOIN to get related information from both tables. Good database design helps us avoid unnecessary repeated data and keeps relationships clear. Before building a project, we should decide what data we need and how it will be stored.\n\nExample:\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE users (\n  id INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  name VARCHAR(100)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE orders (\n  id INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  user_id INT,\n  FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id)\n);\n```\n\nRemember: Primary key identifies a row; foreign key connects related tables.",
