    "category": "C. Database & SQL (46–60)",
    "question_hi": "How to handle async database operations & connection pooling?",
    "answer_hi": "When Node.js communicates with a database, the database operation can take some time. We use asynchronous methods with async/await so JavaScript can continue handling other work while waiting for the database result. For example, await db.execute() waits for the query result inside that async function, but it does not block the entire Node.js application. We also need to handle database errors using try...catch. Another important concept is connection pooling. A connection pool keeps multiple database connections ready for reuse. Without a pool, creating a new connection for every query can add unnecessary work. A pool helps manage connections efficiently, especially when multiple users access the application at the same time.\n\n```javascript\ntry {\n  const [rows] = await db.execute(\n    'SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?',\n    [1]\n  );\n  console.log(rows);\n} catch (error) {\n  console.error('Database query failed');\n}\n```\n\nRemember: Async operations help avoid blocking; connection pools help reuse database connections.",
    "question_en": "How to handle async database operations & connection pooling?",
    "answer_en": "When Node.js communicates with a database, the database operation can take some time. We use asynchronous methods with async/await so JavaScript can continue handling other work while waiting for the database result. For example, await db.execute() waits for the query result inside that async function, but it does not block the entire Node.js application. We also need to handle database errors using try...catch. Another important concept is connection pooling. A connection pool keeps multiple database connections ready for reuse. Without a pool, creating a new connection for every query can add unnecessary work. A pool helps manage connections efficiently, especially when multiple users access the application at the same time.\n\n```javascript\ntry {\n  const [rows] = await db.execute(\n    'SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?',\n    [1]\n  );\n  console.log(rows);\n} catch (error) {\n  console.error('Database query failed');\n}\n```\n\nRemember: Async operations help avoid blocking; connection pools help reuse database connections.",
