    "num": "53",
    "category": "C. Database & SQL (46–60)",
    "question_hi": "What is password hashing and bcrypt?",
    "answer_hi": "Password hashing means converting a password into a hash before storing it in the database. We should never save users' passwords as plain text because anyone who gets access to the database could read them. We can use bcrypt to hash passwords during registration. When the user logs in, we compare the entered password with the stored hash using bcrypt.compare(). We do not decrypt the hash because password hashing is designed to be one-way. Bcrypt also uses a salt to make password hashes harder to attack. We should never send password hashes back to the frontend. Even with hashing, we must protect the database and follow other security practices.\n\nExample:\n```javascript\nconst bcrypt = require('bcrypt');\n\nconst hashedPassword = await bcrypt.hash(password, 10);\n\n// During login\nconst isMatch = await bcrypt.compare(enteredPassword, hashedPassword);\n```\n\nRemember: Registration uses hash(); login uses compare().",
    "question_en": "What is password hashing and bcrypt?",
    "answer_en": "Password hashing means converting a password into a hash before storing it in the database. We should never save users' passwords as plain text because anyone who gets access to the database could read them. We can use bcrypt to hash passwords during registration. When the user logs in, we compare the entered password with the stored hash using bcrypt.compare(). We do not decrypt the hash because password hashing is designed to be one-way. Bcrypt also uses a salt to make password hashes harder to attack. We should never send password hashes back to the frontend. Even with hashing, we must protect the database and follow other security practices.\n\nExample:\n```javascript\nconst bcrypt = require('bcrypt');\n\nconst hashedPassword = await bcrypt.hash(password, 10);\n\n// During login\nconst isMatch = await bcrypt.compare(enteredPassword, hashedPassword);\n```\n\nRemember: Registration uses hash(); login uses compare().",
