    "num": "54",
    "category": "C. Database & SQL (46–60)",
    "question_hi": "What is the difference between Authentication and Authorization?",
    "answer_hi": "Authentication and authorization are two different parts of security. Authentication checks who the user is. For example, when a user logs in with an email and password, the server checks whether the credentials are correct. Authorization checks what the authenticated user is allowed to do. For example, a normal user may view products, but only an admin may delete products or manage other users. In a Node.js application, we can use login logic for authentication and middleware to check permissions for authorization. JWT can help verify a user's identity, while role checks can decide whether the user has permission to access a route. Both are important because logging in should not automatically give every user access to every feature.\n\nExample: A user logs in successfully, so authentication passes. But if that user tries to open an admin-only route, authorization should block the request and return 403 Forbidden.\n\nRemember:\nAuthentication = Who are you?\nAuthorization = What are you allowed to do?",
    "question_en": "What is the difference between Authentication and Authorization?",
    "answer_en": "Authentication and authorization are two different parts of security. Authentication checks who the user is. For example, when a user logs in with an email and password, the server checks whether the credentials are correct. Authorization checks what the authenticated user is allowed to do. For example, a normal user may view products, but only an admin may delete products or manage other users. In a Node.js application, we can use login logic for authentication and middleware to check permissions for authorization. JWT can help verify a user's identity, while role checks can decide whether the user has permission to access a route. Both are important because logging in should not automatically give every user access to every feature.\n\nExample: A user logs in successfully, so authentication passes. But if that user tries to open an admin-only route, authorization should block the request and return 403 Forbidden.\n\nRemember:\nAuthentication = Who are you?\nAuthorization = What are you allowed to do?",
