    "num": "60",
    "category": "C. Database & SQL (46–60)",
    "question_hi": "How to implement pagination, search, and filtering in an API?",
    "answer_hi": "Pagination, search, and filtering help us return useful data without sending every database record at once. Pagination divides results into smaller pages. Search finds records matching a search term, such as a product name. Filtering limits results based on conditions, such as category or price. For example, an e-commerce API may have thousands of products. Instead of returning all products, we can return 10 products per page and let the user search or filter them. In a MySQL application, we can use LIMIT and OFFSET for basic pagination. We should use parameterized queries when inserting user-provided values into SQL queries.\n\nExample:\n```sql\nSELECT id, name, price\nFROM products\nWHERE category = ?\nORDER BY id\nLIMIT ? OFFSET ?;\n```\n\nHere, the category is a filter, LIMIT controls the page size, and OFFSET determines where the results begin. Search can be added using a condition such as name LIKE ?.\n\nRemember: Pagination reduces the amount of data returned, search finds matching records, and filtering narrows results by conditions.",
    "question_en": "How to implement pagination, search, and filtering in an API?",
    "answer_en": "Pagination, search, and filtering help us return useful data without sending every database record at once. Pagination divides results into smaller pages. Search finds records matching a search term, such as a product name. Filtering limits results based on conditions, such as category or price. For example, an e-commerce API may have thousands of products. Instead of returning all products, we can return 10 products per page and let the user search or filter them. In a MySQL application, we can use LIMIT and OFFSET for basic pagination. We should use parameterized queries when inserting user-provided values into SQL queries.\n\nExample:\n```sql\nSELECT id, name, price\nFROM products\nWHERE category = ?\nORDER BY id\nLIMIT ? OFFSET ?;\n```\n\nHere, the category is a filter, LIMIT controls the page size, and OFFSET determines where the results begin. Search can be added using a condition such as name LIKE ?.\n\nRemember: Pagination reduces the amount of data returned, search finds matching records, and filtering narrows results by conditions.",
    "source": "written",
    "topic": "node",
