    "num": "74",
    "category": "D. Node.js Practical Concepts (61–75)",
    "question_hi": "How do you explain your project and debug errors?",
    "answer_hi": "When explaining a backend project, start with the problem it solves, the technologies used, and the main features. Then explain how a request travels through the application. For example, a request comes from the frontend, reaches an Express route, passes through any required middleware, and reaches the controller. The controller may call a service or database layer, and the server sends a response to the client. When debugging, first reproduce the error and read the error message. Then check the request URL, HTTP method, request body, server logs, and database query. Tools such as Postman, the browser's developer tools, and breakpoints can help identify the issue. You should also handle errors properly and avoid exposing sensitive internal details to users.\n\nExample flow:\nFrontend → Express route → Middleware → Controller → Service/Database → Response\n\nRemember: Explain what your project does, how data flows through it, what you personally understand, and how you find and fix errors. Never claim a feature works if you have not tested it.",
    "question_en": "How do you explain your project and debug errors?",
    "answer_en": "When explaining a backend project, start with the problem it solves, the technologies used, and the main features. Then explain how a request travels through the application. For example, a request comes from the frontend, reaches an Express route, passes through any required middleware, and reaches the controller. The controller may call a service or database layer, and the server sends a response to the client. When debugging, first reproduce the error and read the error message. Then check the request URL, HTTP method, request body, server logs, and database query. Tools such as Postman, the browser's developer tools, and breakpoints can help identify the issue. You should also handle errors properly and avoid exposing sensitive internal details to users.\n\nExample flow:\nFrontend → Express route → Middleware → Controller → Service/Database → Response\n\nRemember: Explain what your project does, how data flows through it, what you personally understand, and how you find and fix errors. Never claim a feature works if you have not tested it.",
    "source": "written",
