    "num": "83",
    "category": "E. Express Practical + Project (76–85)",
    "question_hi": "What is a good Node.js project structure (routes, controllers, services)?",
    "answer_hi": "Project structure means how we organize files and folders in an application. In a small project, we may keep most code in one file, but as the project grows, that becomes difficult to maintain. In an Express application, we can separate routes, controllers, services, and database logic. Routes define API endpoints, controllers handle requests and responses, services contain business logic, and the database layer handles database operations. This separation makes code easier to understand, test, and update. For example, a user registration route can call a controller, which calls a service to validate the registration process and save the user. We do not need a complex structure for every small project, but separating responsibilities is useful as the application grows.\n\nExample:\n```\nproject/\n├── routes/\n│   └── userRoutes.js\n├── controllers/\n│   └── userController.js\n├── services/\n│   └── userService.js\n├── config/\n│   └── database.js\n├── app.js\n└── package.json\n```\n\nRemember: Routes define endpoints, controllers handle requests, services contain business logic, and the database layer manages queries.",
    "question_en": "What is a good Node.js project structure (routes, controllers, services)?",
    "answer_en": "Project structure means how we organize files and folders in an application. In a small project, we may keep most code in one file, but as the project grows, that becomes difficult to maintain. In an Express application, we can separate routes, controllers, services, and database logic. Routes define API endpoints, controllers handle requests and responses, services contain business logic, and the database layer handles database operations. This separation makes code easier to understand, test, and update. For example, a user registration route can call a controller, which calls a service to validate the registration process and save the user. We do not need a complex structure for every small project, but separating responsibilities is useful as the application grows.\n\nExample:\n```\nproject/\n├── routes/\n│   └── userRoutes.js\n├── controllers/\n│   └── userController.js\n├── services/\n│   └── userService.js\n├── config/\n│   └── database.js\n├── app.js\n└── package.json\n```\n\nRemember: Routes define endpoints, controllers handle requests, services contain business logic, and the database layer manages queries.",
