    "num": "94",
    "category": "F. Follow-up & Concurrency (86–100)",
    "question_hi": "How does Node.js handle multiple requests? (Node.js Concurrency)",
    "answer_hi": "Node.js uses JavaScript on a main thread and an event loop to manage asynchronous operations. When a request requires I/O, such as a database query or file operation, Node.js can wait for that operation without blocking the main JavaScript thread. While the operation is in progress, Node.js can handle other work and process other requests. When the operation finishes, its callback or Promise continuation can run when the event loop gets to it. Some operations use the operating system, while certain tasks use libuv's thread pool. Node.js can therefore handle many concurrent I/O operations efficiently, even though JavaScript generally executes on one main thread per process. However, CPU-heavy synchronous code can block that thread and delay other requests.\n\nRemember: Node.js handles concurrent I/O efficiently, but long-running synchronous JavaScript can block the event loop.",
    "question_en": "How does Node.js handle multiple requests? (Node.js Concurrency)",
    "answer_en": "Node.js uses JavaScript on a main thread and an event loop to manage asynchronous operations. When a request requires I/O, such as a database query or file operation, Node.js can wait for that operation without blocking the main JavaScript thread. While the operation is in progress, Node.js can handle other work and process other requests. When the operation finishes, its callback or Promise continuation can run when the event loop gets to it. Some operations use the operating system, while certain tasks use libuv's thread pool. Node.js can therefore handle many concurrent I/O operations efficiently, even though JavaScript generally executes on one main thread per process. However, CPU-heavy synchronous code can block that thread and delay other requests.\n\nRemember: Node.js handles concurrent I/O efficiently, but long-running synchronous JavaScript can block the event loop.",
